{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And fire came forth from before HaShem and consumed them, and they died before HaShem.</b> They sinned with fire and were punished with fire. And this death that Scripture mentions — <b>and they died before HaShem</b> — was the separation of the soul from the body: the fire entered into their bodies through their nostrils, and then their souls separated from their bodies. For one cannot say that their souls were burned, God forbid, for the Torah called them those who draw near, as it says (Leviticus 10:3), 'בקרובי אקדש' — 'through those near to Me I am sanctified.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Moses said to Aaron his brother, 'I knew that the House would be sanctified, and I supposed it would be either through me or through you. Now I know that they are greater than me and you.' And there is no doubt that the merit of their souls is great for the World to Come, for their death was an atonement for their sins — therefore their death is mentioned on Yom HaKippurim in the parashah of Aḥarei Mot. And so we find in the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Yoma (1:1): 'Why is the death of Nadav and Avihu mentioned on Yom HaKippurim? To teach you that the death of the righteous atones.' And furthermore one cannot say that their bodies and garments were burned, for Scripture testifies, 'וישאום בכתנותם' — 'and they carried them in their tunics' — for it is the statute of the priesthood to have a tunic, in the manner of what is said (Exodus 28:40): 'ולבני אהרן תעשה כתנות.' And this death Scripture calls 'burning,' as it is written (Leviticus 10:6): 'יבכו את השרפה אשר שרף HaShem' — 'they shall mourn the burning that HaShem burned.'" "<b>And it appears</b> that from here our Rabbis of blessed memory learned the death of burning, which is among the four deaths of the court. And the death of burning was that they would melt lead and open the mouth of the one liable to burning and force-feed it down into his throat, and the innards are burned and his soul departs. For the law of the Torah is not to burn his body, but rather in the likeness of the death of the sons of Aaron at the hand of Heaven, whose bodies were not burned at all. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'Like two threads of fire entered into the two nostrils and their souls departed.' And of this same nature was the death of the army of Sennacherib, concerning which Isaiah of blessed memory prophesied, as it says (Isaiah 9:4): 'כי כל סאון סואן ברעש ושמלה מגוללה בדמים והיתה לשרפה מאכולת אש' — the word סאון is inverted, like אסון; and the meaning of the verse is: in all the other wars, the slain are killed by the crash and tumult of battle, and the garments of the warriors are dyed and rolled in blood; but this war of Sennacherib will be for burning and consumption of fire, and not like the rest of those slain in wars. And this is what is written (II Kings 19:35): 'ויצא מלאך HaShem ויך במחנה אשור מאה ושמונים וחמשה אלף וישכימו בבקר והנה כלם פגרים מתים.' And all this plague came upon Sennacherib through the power of the prayer that Hezekiah and Isaiah prayed toward Heaven, and their prayer was heard — as it is written in Chronicles (II Chronicles 32): 'ויתפלל יחזקיהו המלך וישעיהו בן אמוץ הנביא על זאת ויזעקו השמים, וישלח HaShem מלאך ויכחד כל גבור חיל ונגיד ושר במחנה מלך אשור וישב בבושת פנים לארצו ויבא בית אלהיו ומיוצאי מעיו שם הפילוהו בחרב.' And because their death was a death of burning — their souls were burned from within and they remained intact together with their garments — therefore the prophet said (Isaiah 10:16): 'ותחת כבודו יקד יקוד כיקוד אש,' meaning: a kindling of fire like the kindling of fire of Nadav and Avihu. And he said 'ותחת כבודו' — 'and under his glory' — as if to say 'and under his garments,' as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: R. Yochanan used to call them 'his glorious garments' (מאני מכבדותי), for their garments remained intact, like the garments of Nadav and Avihu, concerning whom Scripture mentions: <b>וישאום בכתנותם.</b> And concerning this the prophet said (ibid.): 'וכבוד יערו וכרמילו' — these are his numerous troops, like the trees of the forest; 'מנפש ועד בשר יכלה והיה כמסוס נוסס' — meaning that the glory of his forest mentioned will be like the סס, which is a worm that eats away at wood from within and leaves the bark intact on the outside, as it says (Isaiah 51:8): 'וכצמר יאכלם סס.' And the intention of this is that it burned their souls and left their bodies and garments intact. And this army was of the sons of Japheth, who had the merit of garments, as a reward for what is written (Genesis 9:23): 'ויקח שם ויפת את השמלה' — and Shem, of whom we are descendants, merited on this account the commandment of tzitzit. And the word 'אותם,' being plene (with a vav), alludes to the sixth fire of the Shekhinah, for so they said in tractate Yoma: there are six fires — fire that consumes but does not drink, drinks but does not consume, consumes and drinks, consumes moist and dry, fire that repels fire, fire that consumes fire. Consumes but does not drink — that is ours; drinks but does not consume — that of ḥullin; consumes and drinks — that of Elijah; consumes moist and dry — that of the altar arrangement; fire that repels fire — that of Gabriel; fire that consumes fire — that of the Shekhinah, as the Master said: He thrust His finger among them and burned them. And furthermore there is in it an allusion to the six opinions that came before our Rabbis of blessed memory regarding their sin."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:42.830970317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}